[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Writing Custom C++ and CUDA Extensions in PyTorch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"While PyTorch provides a rich set of operators, there are times when you need to write custom operations to optimize performance or implement novel algorithms that are not natively supported. This tutorial covers how to write, compile, and integrate custom C++ and CUDA extensions into your PyTorch workflows using setuptools and torch.utils.cpp_extension."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before starting, ensure you have a compatible C++ compiler (g++ or MSVC) and the NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit installed, matching the CUDA version PyTorch was built with."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Writing the C++ Extension"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, we will write a simple C++ function that performs scaling on a PyTorch tensor. We use torch.h to interact with tensors and Pybind11 to expose the function to Python."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"#include <torch/extension.h>

torch::Tensor custom_scale_cpu(torch::Tensor tensor, float factor) {
    return tensor * factor;
}

PYBIND11_MODULE(TORCH_EXTENSION_NAME, m) {
    m.def("custom_scale_cpu", &custom_scale_cpu, "Custom CPU scaling function");
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Writing the CUDA Extension"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, we implement the same scaling operation using a CUDA kernel for parallel execution on the GPU."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"#include <torch/extension.h>
#include <cuda.h>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

__global__ void scale_kernel(const float* __restrict__ input, float* __restrict__ output, float factor, int size) {
    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (idx < size) {
        output[idx] = input[idx] * factor;
    }
}

torch::Tensor custom_scale_cuda(torch::Tensor tensor, float factor) {
    auto output = torch::empty_like(tensor);
    int size = tensor.numel();
    const int threads = 256;
    const int blocks = (size + threads - 1) / threads;

    scale_kernel<<<blocks, threads>>>(tensor.data_ptr<float>(), output.data_ptr<float>(), factor, size);
    return output;
}

PYBIND11_MODULE(TORCH_EXTENSION_NAME, m) {
    m.def("custom_scale_cuda", &custom_scale_cuda, "Custom CUDA scaling function");
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Compiling with Setup.py"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To compile both the C++ and CUDA code, we create a setup.py script utilizing PyTorch's CppExtension and CUDAExtension utilities."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"from setuptools import setup
from torch.utils.cpp_extension import BuildExtension, CUDAExtension

setup(
    name='custom_cpp_cuda_extension',
    ext_modules=[
        CUDAExtension(
            'custom_ext',
            ['extension.cpp', 'extension_kernel.cu'],
        )
    ],
    cmdclass={
        'build_ext': BuildExtension
    }
)"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Using the Extension in PyTorch"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once compiled using 'python setup.py install', you can import your custom extension directly in Python just like any other PyTorch module."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import torch
import custom_ext

# Create a tensor on GPU
x = torch.randn(10, device='cuda')

# Call the custom CUDA extension
y = custom_ext.custom_scale_cuda(x, 2.0)
print(y)"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always validate tensor contiguousness using tensor.contiguous() before passing raw pointers to CUDA kernels."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use torch.utils.cpp_extension.load() for Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation during prototyping instead of writing a full setup.py."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle device and dtype checks explicitly inside your C++ bindings to provide meaningful error messages to users."}]
